      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Spanish colonizers first named the area La Punta Concha in 1775, later calling it Beacon Point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayview%E2%80%93Hunters_Point%2C_San_Francisco",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Great Migration, African American workers flocked to Hunters Point’s shipyards, reshaping the neighborhood’s labor force.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayview%E2%80%93Hunters_Point%2C_San_Francisco",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 2016 Olympic bid for San Francisco included an Olympic Village in Bayview–Hunters Point, but the plan was later dropped.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayview%E2%80%93Hunters_Point%2C_San_Francisco",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 1987 mural titled “Tazuri Watu” by artist Brooke Fancher still covers a building at 3rd and Palou.",
